    "content": {
      "component": "blog_article",
      "sharepoint_id": "98",
      "title": "Designing Inclusive Futures for Women and Girls",
      "description": "",
      "date": "Feb 19, 2026",
      "read_time": "2 min read",
      "author": "Farhana Alarakhiya",
      "isGlobal": true,
      "banner_image": {
        "fieldtype": "asset",
        "_local": "98-Banner.png"
      },
      "thumbnail": {
        "fieldtype": "asset"
      },
      "languages": [],
      "show_share": true,
      "show_similar": true,
      "similar_show_header": true,
      "category": "",
      "isFeatured": null,
      "body": [
        {
          "component": "rich_text",
          "content": {
            "type": "doc",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "This International Day for Women and Girls in Science, the UN calls on us to synergize AI, social science, STEM, and finance to build inclusive futures for women and girls."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "But in low- and middle-income countries (LMICs), inclusion is not created by language. It is created by design."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "I am an electrical engineer. In my graduating class, there were 98 boys and 2 girls. Later, as a professional in software and data, I was - more often than not - the only female executive at the table. At global meetings, men have walked up to me and asked if I could get them coffee."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Not because they knew me."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "But because they assumed."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "These assumptions are not harmless. They are embedded in classrooms, boardrooms, hiring decisions, and research funding. And they start early."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Women make up only one in three researchers globally, and less than a third of STEM professionals across Africa. In many Sub-Saharan African countries, fewer than 30% of engineering graduates are women. And yet, when girls are given equal access and encouragement, they perform as well as - and often outperform - boys in math and science."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The problem is not ability."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "It is access. It is expectation. It is power."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "In many LMIC settings, girls are taught - subtly and explicitly - what is \"appropriate.\" They are conditioned to choose differently long before they understand their own potential."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The convergence of AI, STEM, finance, and social science is shaping the next generation of opportunity - and we are entering the AI era with a choice to make."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "And here is the uncomfortable truth:"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "AI will not correct inequity."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "AI will not democratise opportunity unless we design for it."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "AI will codify the biases of the past - at scale."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Because if the data doesn't see women, the system won't serve women."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "At Aga Khan University, I lead teams building AI systems to improve outcomes for the communities we serve. That means being intentional. It means designing systems that account for women's realities, health needs, economic roles, and lived experiences - not as edge cases, but as core users."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "\"Building inclusive futures is not about charity.\"",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "bold"
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "It is about widening the architecture of opportunity."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Fund girls' education. Promote women into decision-making. Audit the data shaping your systems."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "When we give girls access, encouragement, and representation, we do not lose ground - we expand it. We gain stronger institutions, smarter systems, and more resilient societies."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "So, this International Day for Women and Girls in Science, my question is simple:"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "How will you widen the architecture of opportunity?",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "bold"
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
